<p>Israel and Lebanon agreed to extend a U.S.-mediated cessation of hostilities by 45 days after two days of talks in Washington on May 14 and 15, the U.S. State Department said Friday. The truce, which President Trump announced on April 16, had been due to expire Sunday, and State Department spokesman Tommy Pigott said the extension was intended “to enable further progress”. The agreement followed a third round of contacts between Israeli and Lebanese representatives, even as fighting continued in southern Lebanon between Israel and Hezbollah, which was not part of the diplomacy. Lebanese authorities said Israeli strikes since the ceasefire took effect in mid-April had killed more than 400 people in Lebanon; Israel says its operations target Hezbollah infrastructure and that Hezbollah attacks have continued. The State Department said political negotiations will resume June 2-3, while a separate security track involving military delegations will begin at the Pentagon on May 29. As the Washington talks ended, Israeli strikes around Tyre wounded at least 37 people, including hospital personnel, women and children, Lebanese authorities said; Israel’s military said it was striking Hezbollah infrastructure sites in the Tyre area.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/Y5ECH_RiHe4AI5j-salgKQ_n2y7Rx5WkNlobSbGgcqOogD_b2LLTojZF1XVyjdj0YsrKqphIuaeKs0ghBx4p_NskiFjJ5G197H6Q1aH9HT_ExNF18mzke1JiQqEYVGRF0NARXf8jHflokeddWRmzMgt37e75jjfzfGGPnxxy0c8wZQE4uh2317n6447SHSBKe7h3eXDIvf5tkQedeZUwdPuxSKqjgYyfPL9p-ig3BL2l4Zm26nYmBIdSbRmfZRB36LhDzXE9IUlM3DOpMg' alt='The U.S. hosted two days of talks between Israel and Lebanon.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Hanouiyeh strike: A later strike hit a civil defence centre in Hanouiyeh, near Tyre, killing at least six people, including three paramedics, and wounding more than 20.</li><li>Negotiating positions: Lebanon has sought a halt to hostilities, while Israel says Hezbollah must be disarmed as part of any broader agreement.</li><li>Israeli deployment: Israeli forces remain stationed in a self-declared security zone in southern Lebanon, where clashes have continued despite the truce.</li></ul><blockquote>We hope these discussions will advance lasting peace between the two countries, full recognition of each other&#x27;s sovereignty and territorial integrity, and establishing genuine security along their shared border - Tommy Pigott</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>U.S. State Department: The department called the Washington round “highly productive” and described the talks as part of an effort to settle decades of conflict between the countries. (<a href='https://minutemirror.com.pk/israel-lebanon-extend-ceasefire-for-45-days-after-washington-negotiations-556376/'>Minute Mirror</a>)</li><li>Lebanese negotiating delegation: The delegation said the extension and a U.S.-backed security process would provide “critical breathing space for our citizens” and help reinforce state institutions. (<a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/05/15/lebanon-says-truce-extension-with-israel-and-us-backed-security-create-opportunity-for-lasting-stability_6753500_4.html'>Le Monde</a>)</li><li>Israeli position: Israel said the new strikes were not subject to the truce as Washington announced expanded political-settlement talks. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/middle-east/article/3353777/lebanon-and-israel-extend-ceasefire-45-days'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li><li>United Nations humanitarian coordinator Imran Riza: Riza warned that continuing strikes were exacting “an unacceptable toll on civilians and civilian infrastructure.” (<a href='https://tribune.com.pk/story/2608426/lebanon-truce-extended-by-45-days'>The Express Tribune</a>)</li><li>Israel’s ambassador to the U.S. Yechiel Leiter: Leiter said after the talks that ensuring Israel’s security would be critical and wrote that the potential for success was great despite expected setbacks. (<a href='https://www.channelstv.com/20